}}{{Economy of Balisca}}

'''[[Balisca]]''' is a {{W|Developed country|high income country}} with a {{W|post-growth}} and {{W|post-work society|post-work oriented}} economy. The country's unique economic model—often termed "market mutualism" or "cooperative socialism"—represents a distinctive synthesis of [[W:Market mechanism|market mechanisms]] with [[W:Socialism|socialist]] ownership structures, positioning it as an alternative to both [[W:Neoliberal capitalism|neoliberal capitalism]] and [[W:State socialism|state socialism]]. The economy consists of {{W|state-owned enterprises}} (SOEs; known as [[Essential industry|essential industries]]), domestic {{W|cooperative|cooperatives}} and [[W:Cooperative federations|cooperative federations]], as well as a limited foreign businesses in a system of [[W:Planned economy|decentralized planning]], often described internationally as [[W:Market socialism|market socialism]].  

Publicly-owned enterprises accounted for over 60% of Balisca's market capitalization in 2019 and generated 40% of Balisca's GDP of {{I$|link=yes}}$6.002 trillion dollars (9.033 trillion sol) in 2022, with domestic cooperatives (45%) and foreign private businesses and investment (15%) accounting for the remainder. It is the fifth-largest economy in the world by both [[W:Gross domestic product|nominal]] and [[w:Purchasing power parity|purchasing power parity]] (PPP) measurements. 

Balisca is a founding member of the [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO), an {{W|economic union}} committed to the establishment of an international cooperative system as an {{W|alter-globalization|alternative}} to the current system of {{W|corporate globalization}}, through the implementation of {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative economic ideals}}, such as [[W:Solidarity economy|economic solidarity]], {{W|mutualism (economic theory)|mutualism}}, {{W|economic democracy}} and {{W|global justice movement|global justice}}. 

The Baliscan currency is the [[Baliscan sol]] (S/). Balisca is one of Abyala's most stable and prosperous nations, and is one of the leading nations in [[W:Human development|human development]], [[W:Income equality|income equality]], [[Social Development Index|social development]], [[W:Globalization|globalization]], and low perception of corruption. Balisca tops lists in the region in regards to [[Human Development Index|HDI]] and [[W:Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index|inequality-adjusted HDI]], based on its economy and regulatory environment, earning .913 and .889 respectively, placing first out of all countries listed by a wide margin. Balisca has been considered by the World Bank as a [[W:High-income economy|high-income economy]] since July 1964. In 2022, around 1.67% of the population was estimated to live on less than {{I$}}45 (the national [[W:Poverty line|poverty line]]) a day.

Balisca has one of the highest degrees of [[W:Economic freedom#Socialist viewpoints|economic freedom]] of all Abyalan countries, [[Index of Economic Freedom#2025 Index|ranking 17th worldwide]], owing to its [[W:Circular economy|circular]] [[W:Doughnut (economic model)|doughnut economic model]] which strives towards [[W:Right to an adequate standard of living|universal access to a just standard of living]]. This has resulted in an excess of regulation that heavily discourages the presence of [[W:Multinational corporation|multinational corporations]] in favor of small and medium sized cooperatives, which has also translated into lower incoming investment. Balisca is considered the "land of [[W:Cooperative|cooperatives]]" with the twenty largest [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federations]] accounting for around 5% of GDP in 2022. 

== Overview ==
Balisca's economy operates under a constitutional framework that establishes [[W:Economic democracy|economic democracy]] as a fundamental principle. The [[Basic Laws of Balisca]] guarantee workers' rights to [[W:Workers' self-management|self-manage]] productive enterprises, mandate [[W:Communal ownership|communal ownership]] of land and natural resources, and require that [[Essential industry|essential industries]] operate under public or cooperative control. This framework has produced an economy where approximately 80% of enterprises are organized as [[W:Worker cooperative|worker cooperatives]], [[W:Consumer cooperative|consumer cooperatives]], or [[W:State-owned enterprise|state-owned enterprises]], with the remaining 20% comprising small-scale private proprietorships and artisanal businesses.

The Baliscan economy demonstrates significant divergence between [[purchasing power parity]] and [[W:Nominal GDP|nominal]] measurements—a ratio of 1.86:1, among the highest globally for advanced economies. This disparity reflects the deliberate structuring of domestic prices through communal land tenure, cooperative production, and [[W:Price stabilization|price stabilization]] mechanisms that decouple the cost of living from international market valuations.

== Historical development ==
{{Main|Economic history of Balisca}}
=== Colonial and early independence period (1508–1920) ===
The foundations of Balisca's modern economy were laid during the Volisanian colonial period, when the extraction of [[silver]], [[gold]], and [[copper]] from the northern highlands generated substantial wealth while creating deeply unequal social structures. The [[Encomienda|''encomienda'']] system and later [[W:Latifundia|latifundia]] agricultural estates concentrated land ownership among a small colonial elite, while indigenous and mixed-race populations provided labor under coercive conditions.

Following independence in 1808, Balisca's economy remained oriented toward [[W:Primary commodity|primary commodity]] exports—minerals, [[W:Coffee|coffee]], [[W:Sugar|sugar]], and later [[W:Saltpeter|saltpeter]]—while manufacturing remained underdeveloped. The [[Baliscan Gilded Age|Gilded Age]] of the 1870s–1890s saw substantial foreign investment, particularly from [[Breisland]] and [[Hersatia]], in mining and railway infrastructure, but contributed to [[W:Dependency theory|economic dependency]] and regional inequality between the industrializing coastal regions and the agrarian interior.

=== The Baliscan Revolution and economic transformation (1920–1950) ===
The [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1920–1923 fundamentally restructured economic relations. Revolutionary [[W:Anarcho-syndicalism|anarcho-syndicalist]] and [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] movements, led by the [[Confederación Nacional del Trabajo]], seized factories, mines, and agricultural estates, establishing worker-controlled enterprises. The post-revolutionary government formalized these arrangements through the [[Economic Constitution of 1925]], which:

* Abolished private ownership of land, minerals, and water resources
* Established worker cooperatives as the default enterprise form
* Reformed the [[Bank of Balisca]] as a public utility
* Mandated workplace democracy in all enterprises above ten employees
* Established the [[Communal Land Registry]] and abolished the property market

This period saw initial economic disruption as traditional capital fled the country and foreign governments (especially those in Illypnia and [[Lurandia]]) imposed sanctions. However, the [[Popular economic council|popular economic councils]]—local bodies coordinating production and distribution—maintained essential services and gradually restored productive capacity. By 1935, industrial output had recovered to pre-revolutionary levels, and by 1945, the economy had achieved substantial diversification.

=== Post-war development and the Special Period (1950–1983) ===
{{Main|Baliscan miracle|Special Period}}

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period brought sustained growth averaging 5.2% annually from 1950 to 1973, driven by state-led industrialization, infrastructure development, and the expansion of cooperative enterprises. The [[National Rail Modernization Act of 1962]] exemplified this approach, establishing the world's second high-speed rail network after [[Andalus]]'s pioneering system.

The successive crises in the [[Camboriú War|Marañón]] and [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis|Bight of Ayorin]] and subsequent economic disruption of the [[October 76|October 76 movement]] initiated the [[Special Period]] (1976–1983), during which Balisca faced severe challenges including energy shortages, food insecurity, and declining terms of trade. The government response—characterized by [[W:Food sovereignty|food sovereignty]] policies, [[W:Organic agriculture|organic agricultural]] conversion, transportation rationalization, and [[W:Energy transition|energy transition]]—transformed the economy's resource efficiency while significantly reducing living standards temporarily.

=== Contemporary period (1983–present) ===
Since emerging from the Special Period, Balisca has pursued a development path emphasizing [[W:Degrowth|qualitative development]] over quantitative expansion. The economy has transitioned toward [[W:Knowledge economy|knowledge-intensive]] sectors, [[W:Renewable energy|renewable energy]], and [[W:Social services|social services]], while maintaining strong industrial capacity in strategic sectors. The [[Social Development Index|Environmental and Social Accounting Framework]] adopted in 1991 replaced GDP growth as the primary measure of economic success, emphasizing [[W:Human development|human development]], [[W:Ecological sustainability|ecological sustainability]], and [[W:Social equity|social equity]] metrics.

== Economic structure ==

=== Ownership patterns ===
The Baliscan economy is characterized by three primary ownership forms:

Worker cooperatives operate under the principle of [[W:One member, one vote|one member, one vote]], with surplus distributed according to labor contribution rather than capital ownership. The [[Cooperative Code of 1934]] established standardized frameworks for cooperative governance, external auditing, and inter-cooperative federation. State-owned enterprises function as [[W:Public utility|public utilities]], with mandates to provide essential goods and services at cost while maintaining employment and regional development objectives.

=== Sectoral composition ===
The Baliscan economy demonstrates a three-sector structure typical of advanced economies, with distinctive internal characteristics:

'''Primary sector (5.7% of GDP)''': Dominated by [[cooperative agriculture]] and [[state-owned mining]]. Agricultural policy emphasizes [[food sovereignty]] over export orientation, with production quotas and price guarantees ensuring domestic food security. Mining operations, managed by the [[Baliscan Mining Corporation]], prioritize domestic industrial needs and long-term resource stewardship over maximum extraction.

'''Secondary sector (29.3% of GDP)''': Characterized by [[flexible manufacturing]] networks, [[cooperative industrial groups]], and [[strategic industries]] under state coordination. The manufacturing sector emphasizes [[durability]], [[repairability]], and [[resource efficiency]] over planned obsolescence, reflecting constitutional prohibitions on anti-consumerist practices.

'''Tertiary sector (65.0% of GDP)''': Includes [[social services]] (healthcare, education, care work) operated primarily through public and cooperative providers, [[financial services]] provided by the [[Baliscan Central Bank]] and cooperative credit unions, and [[knowledge services]] (research, design, consulting) emerging as growth sectors.

