== Monetary and fiscal policy ==
{{Main|Bank of Balisca|Taxation in Balisca}}

=== Currency and monetary system ===
The [[Baliscan sol]] (S/) serves as the national currency, managed by the [[Bank of Balisca]] (BdB) as a public utility rather than an independent central bank. The BCB operates under a dual mandate of [[W:Price stability|price stability]] and [[W:Full employment|full employment]], with explicit authorization to finance public investment and cooperative development through [[W:Sovereign money|sovereign money]] creation.

The currency maintains a [[managed float]] within the [[COCO Currency Cooperation Mechanism]], which coordinates exchange rates among member states. The sol's exchange rate is deliberately maintained below market-clearing levels to preserve export competitiveness and domestic price stability, contributing to the PPP-nominal GDP gap.

=== Banking and finance ===
The financial sector operates under [[W:Public utility|public utility]] principles, with the BCB providing:

* '''Retail banking services''' through the [[Popular Bank Network]]
* '''Development financing''' through the [[Industrial Development Bank]]
* '''Agricultural credit''' through the [[Agrarian Credit Bank]]
* '''Housing finance''' through the [[Housing Bank of Balisca]]

Private banking is prohibited, while [[W:Credit union|credit unions]] and [[W:Cooperative bank|cooperative banks]] operate under strict regulation limiting interest rates and speculative activities. The [[Financial Transactions Tax]] of 0.5% on all financial transfers generates significant revenue while discouraging high-frequency trading.

=== Fiscal framework ===
Taxation in Balisca operates through a combination of:

* '''Land value capture''': As all land is communally owned, users pay [[W:Ground rent|ground rent]] based on unimproved land value, calculated and collected by [[communal land committees]]
* '''Resource royalties''': A 15% levy on mineral and resource extraction, allocated to the [[National Development Fund]]
* '''Income surcharges''': Progressive assessments on above-average incomes, with no taxation below the median income
* '''Consumption taxes''': A 12% value-added tax with exemptions for essential goods
* '''Wealth assessments''': Annual levies on net worth above {{INTL$}}500,000

The fiscal system operates on the principle of [[W:Fiscal federalism|fiscal federalism]], with significant revenue collection and expenditure authority devolved to [[Cantons of Balisca#Political structure and role|cantonal governments]] and [[Communes of Balisca|communes]].

== Labor and employment ==
{{Main|Labor relations in Balisca|Trade unions in Balisca}}

=== Employment structure ===
Balisca's labor market is characterized by:

* '''High unionization''': 90% of workers belong to [[sectoral unions]] or [[W:Industrial union|industrial unions]]
* '''Workplace democracy''': All enterprises above ten workers must establish [[W:Workers' council|workers' councils]] with binding authority over working conditions
* '''Short working hours''': The standard workweek is 24 hours over four days, established by the [[Work Time Reduction Act of 2018]]
* '''Job security''': Dismissal requires approval by workers' councils and is subject to appeal through [[Labor tribunal|labor tribunals]]

=== Wage and compensation systems ===
Compensation in cooperative enterprises follows the [[labor contribution principle]], where members receive shares of surplus proportional to hours worked, skill coefficients, and seniority. The [[Equitable Society Compact]] of 1983 establishes that no member may receive more than five times the share of the lowest-paid member within any cooperative.

The [[Basic Pay Initiative]] guarantees a [[W:Living wage|living wage]] floor for all workers, including non-citizens, updated quarterly based on regional cost-of-living indices. [[W:Social wage|Social wage]] provisions—including universal healthcare, housing, education, and food subsidies—supplement monetary compensation.

=== Labor participation in governance ===
Workers exercise significant influence over economic policy through:

* '''Sectoral representation''': Union representatives hold reserved seats on the boards of state-owned enterprises
* '''Economic planning councils''': Regional and confederal bodies include mandated worker representation
* '''Workplace governance''': Workers' councils exercise authority over production decisions, hiring, and enterprise strategy

== Trade and external relations ==
<nowiki>{{Main|Foreign trade of Balisca|COCO]]</nowiki>

=== International trade patterns ===
Balisca's trade policy emphasizes [[import substitution]] for essential goods and [[export orientation]] for strategic commodities. Major exports include:

* [[Lithium]] and [[rare earth elements]] (28% of export value)
* [[Semiconductor|Semiconductors]] and [[electronics]] (22%)
* [[Agricultural products]] (15%)
* [[Manufactured goods]] (18%)
* [[Services]] (17%)

Major imports include [[petroleum]] (for petrochemicals), specialized machinery, and certain consumer goods. The [[Trade Balance Commission]] maintains authority to restrict imports that threaten domestic industries or violate environmental and labor standards.

=== COCO and economic integration ===
As a founding member of the [[Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO), Balisca participates in a [[preferential trade area]] and [[monetary cooperation mechanism]] with member states. COCO operates on principles of [[complementary trade]], [[technology sharing]], and [[development finance]], distinguishing it from conventional free trade agreements.

The [[COCO Development Bank]], headquartered in [[Castejón]], provides financing for infrastructure and industrial development across member states. The [[COCO Clearing Union]] facilitates multilateral trade settlement without requiring convertibility to reserve currencies.

== Social and environmental accounting ==
{{Main|Environmental accounting in Balisca|Social accounting in Balisca}}

=== Beyond GDP measurement ===
Since 1991, Balisca has measured economic performance through the [[Environmental and Social Accounting Framework]] (ESAF), which tracks:

* '''Human Development Index''': Standardized health, education, and income measures
* '''Ecological Footprint''': Resource consumption relative to biocapacity
* '''Gini coefficient''': Income and wealth inequality
* '''Social Cohesion Index''': Measures of community, trust, and participation
* '''Democratic Participation Index''': Levels of engagement in governance

The ESAF replaces GDP growth as the primary target of economic policy, with the [[Confederal Development Plan]] establishing multi-year targets across all indicators.

=== Environmental economics ===
Balisca's economic policy incorporates [[W:Ecological economics|ecological economics]] principles through:

* '''Carbon budgeting''': A national carbon budget caps emissions, with allocations distributed to regions and enterprises
* '''Resource accounting''': Natural resource degradation is recorded as economic cost, not income
* '''Extended producer responsibility''': Manufacturers bear lifetime responsibility for products, including disposal
* '''Circular economy mandates''': Industries must achieve minimum [[recycling]] and [[reuse]] rates

== Challenges and future prospects ==
{{Main|Economic challenges in Balisca}}

=== Contemporary challenges ===
The Baliscan economy faces several structural challenges:

* '''Demographic transition''': An aging population and below-replacement fertility create pressure on pension and healthcare systems
* '''Housing shortage''': Rapid urbanization and immigration have outpaced housing construction, creating a [[Baliscan housing shortage|chronic deficit]]
* '''Investment insufficiency''': Restrictions on capital inflows and profit repatriation limit productive investment
* '''Regional inequality''': Despite equalization transfers, coastal-industrial regions significantly outperform rural-agricultural areas
* '''Technology transition''': Adapting the cooperative model to emerging technologies and platform economics

=== Future development trajectory ===
The [[Confederal Development Plan 2025–2035]] establishes priorities including:

* '''Housing expansion''': Construction of 550,000 units annually through cooperative and public housing
* '''Energy transition''': Achievement of 100% renewable electricity by 2030
* '''Digital transformation''': Extension of cooperative principles to digital platforms and AI-mediated services
* '''Regional rebalancing''': Investment in rural infrastructure and development
* '''Demographic policy''': Expanded childcare, immigration facilitation, and elder care systems

